![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

In Python, negative indexing is supported, so if `line_num` is `0` (due to malformed input or parsing edge cases), `line_num - 1` becomes `-1`. This will silently access the last element of `line_offsets` (the end of the file offset) instead of raising an error or handling it gracefully, leading to incorrect character offsets. Additionally, if `line_num` exceeds the number of lines, an `IndexError` will be raised.

Adding a guard to ensure `line_num` is within the valid range `1 <= line_num < len(line_offsets)` prevents these issues.

```suggestion
def line_col_to_char(line_offsets: list[int], line_num: int, col_num: int) -> int:
    """Convert 1-based line/col to a 0-based character offset."""
    if not (1 <= line_num < len(line_offsets)):
        return col_num - 1
    return line_offsets[line_num - 1] + (col_num - 1)
```

